ce\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H14N4O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-5-guanidinopentanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"arginine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C6H14N4O2/c7-4(5(11)12)2-1-3-10-6(8)9/h4H,1-3,7H2,(H,11,12)(H4,8,9,10)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrocyanation of acetylene to acrylonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic cyanosulfidic evaporitic pool or shoreline film; concentration by evaporation and local UV shielding favor survival of HCN adducts.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"HCN adds across the C-C triple bond of acetylene to give the vinyl nitrile acrylonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the documented entry step of the cyanosulfidic arginine sequence and efficiently converts two plausible atmospheric or volcanic feedstocks into a three-carbon activated nitrile (Patel et al., 2015). In surface settings the main challenge is maintaining sufficient local HCN concentration while limiting destructive UV side chemistry.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Michael addition of ammonia to acrylonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Mildly basic, NH3-rich cyanosulfidic pool under wet-dry cycling.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Conjugate 1,4-addition of ammonia to the beta-carbon of acrylonitrile yields the beta-amino nitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) place this Michael addition directly after acrylonitrile formation in the arginine sequence. The step is chemically favorable because acrylonitrile is an activated Michael acceptor, though hydrolysis and oligomerization remain plausible side reactions in surface pools.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Guanidination of 3-aminopropanenitrile with cyanamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Wet-dry concentration enhances cyanamide capture by the amino nitrile.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The primary amine attacks cyanamide and forms the guanidine-amidine linkage characteristic of arginine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the decisive arginine-specific functionalization in the Patel et al. (2015) network because it installs the guanidino motif absent from other amino acid routes. The literature review also highlights uncertainty over sustained prebiotic cyanamide concentrations, so this is a plausible supply bottleneck.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Dry Cu/H2S cyclization to 4-hydroxy-2-iminohexahydropyrimidine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"elemental copper\", \"hydrogen sulfide\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu/H2S redox system\",\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"Partially dry anoxic mineral film on Cu-bearing grains in an H2S-rich surface setting.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Intramolecular attack of a guanidine nitrogen on the nitrile closes a six-membered ring; Cu/H2S assists formation of the cyclic hemiaminal.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is one of the three experimentally reported Cu/H2S-assisted cyclization variants in Patel et al. (2015). The dry variant is geochemically sensible in evaporitic films where concentration and reduced copper can promote intramolecular attack, although early-Earth access to native copper remains debated.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolytic Cu/H2S cyclization to 4-hydroxy-2-iminohexahydropyrimidine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"elemental copper\", \"hydrogen sulfide\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu/H2S redox system\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Shallow anoxic surface pool with Cu-bearing grains and dissolved sulfide.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The same intramolecular ring closure occurs as in the dry variant, but water assists nitrile hydration to the C4 hydroxylated hemiaminal.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) also reported a hydrolytic Cu/H2S cyclization variant. Keeping it separate preserves the experimentally observed ambiguity between dry-film and aqueous ring closure under cyanosulfidic surface conditions.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolytic Cu/H2S cyclization with ammonia release\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"elemental copper\", \"hydrogen sulfide\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu/H2S redox system\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Shallow anoxic surface pool with Cu-bearing grains and dissolved sulfide.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Water-assisted ring closure and hydration produce the cyclic hemiaminal with net NH3 release.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This third documented cyclization variant from Patel et al. (2015) differs by net ammonia release and therefore creates an internal nitrogen recycle within the network. Like the other cyclizations, however, it depends on Cu/H2S conditions whose geochemical availability may have been patchy.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"HCN ring opening of the hexahydropyrimidine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"HCN-rich anoxic pool intermittently concentrated by evaporation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide attacks the hemiaminal carbon, opens the ring, and generates a linear cyanohydrin-guanidine.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) used HCN to reopen the cyclic hemiaminal and thereby homologate the carbon skeleton. This is a crucial step, but the review notes that regioselectivity of the C4 opening is still not fully resolved.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Nitrile thiolysis to the hydroxy-thioamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Sulfide-rich surface water with limited oxidants and reduced sulfur input from volcanic or geothermal sources.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydrogen sulfide adds across the nitrile to form the corresponding thioamide while retaining the adjacent hydroxyl group.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The nitrile-to-thioamide conversion is a hallmark cyanosulfidic transformation and was experimentally demonstrated in the arginine route by Patel et al. (2015). Sulfide-rich surface waters make the chemistry plausible, though the reactive cyanohydrin and thioamide intermediates would benefit from partial UV shielding.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Reductive dehydroxylation of the hydroxy-thioamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"elemental copper\", \"hydrogen sulfide\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu/H2S redox system\",\n        \"aqueous\": false,\n        \"other\": \"Dry-down or mineral-film phase on Cu-bearing grains in H2S-rich conditions.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cu/H2S-mediated reductive elimination removes the hydroxyl group and yields the deoxygenated thioamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) showed Cu/H2S-mediated reductive dehydroxylation of the hydroxy-thioamide. This is a key carbon-skeleton editing step, but it is also yield-sensitive because it depends on the contested availability of reduced copper at the surface.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Thioamide-to-nitrile exchange to 2-(4-cyano-4-hydroxybutyl)guanidine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"elemental copper\", \"hydrogen sulfide\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu/H2S redox system\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"HCN recharge during the wet phase of cyanosulfidic pool cycling.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Thioamide hydrolysis-exchange coupled to HCN installation of a new nitrile regenerates a terminal cyanohydrin and releases ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The complex thioamide-to-nitrile and cyanohydrin exchange was reported by Patel et al. (2015) and exemplifies the nitrile-thioamide redox cycling of cyanosulfidic chemistry. Because the mechanistic sequence is multistage, this is plausibly one of the more selectivity-limited yield bottlenecks in the network.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Amination of the cyanohydrin-guanidine to the alpha-aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"NH3-rich concentrated pool after evaporation of ammonium-bearing solutions.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonolysis of the cyanohydrin center converts the hydroxyl-bearing nitrile into the alpha-aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) placed this amination late in the arginine sequence because it directly installs the alpha-amino group needed for the amino acid while leaving the guanidine side chain intact. The step is chemically attractive because it avoids requiring a separate prebiotic transamination chemistry.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Aqueous hydrolysis of the alpha-aminonitrile to arginine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Repeated heating/cooling and wet-dry cycling promote hydration and hydrolysis; no chiral selection is implied.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Stepwise nitrile hydration and hydrolysis, via an amide-level intermediate, converts the aminonitrile into the amino acid and releases ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) completed the sequence by aqueous nitrile hydrolysis to arginine. The chemistry is standard for aminonitriles, but it should furnish racemic arginine rather than enantioenriched L-arginine, and the literature highlights low cumulative multistep yield as a major accumulation challenge.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Complete cyanosulfidic route via dry cyclization\",\n      \"description\": \"Complete experimentally supported feedstock-to-target route from acetylene, HCN, NH3, cyanamide, and H2S using the dry Cu/H2S cyclization variant.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Complete cyanosulfidic route via hydrolytic cyclization\",\n      \"description\": \"Complete experimentally supported feedstock-to-target route using the hydrolytic Cu/H2S cyclization variant.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Complete cyanosulfidic route via hydrolytic cyclization with NH3 release\",\n      \"description\": \"Complete experimentally supported feedstock-to-target route using the hydrolytic Cu/H2S cyclization variant that recycles ammonia.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Guanidine-hub route via dry cyclization\",\n      \"description\": \"Experimentally observed downstream route from 2-(2-cyanoethyl)guanidine to arginine using the dry cyclization branch.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Guanidine-hub route via hydrolytic cyclization\",\n      \"description\": \"Experimentally observed downstream route from 2-(2-cyanoethyl)guanidine to arginine using the hydrolytic cyclization branch.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Guanidine-hub route via hydrolytic cyclization with NH3 release\",\n      \"description\": \"Experimentally observed downstream route from 2-(2-cyanoethyl)guanidine to arginine using the ammonia-releasing hydrolytic cyclization branch.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Hexahydropyrimidine to arginine\",\n      \"description\": \"Experimentally observed late-stage route from the cyclic hexahydropyrimidine intermediate to arginine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Ring-opened cyanohydrin to arginine\",\n      \"description\": \"Experimentally observed late-stage route from the ring-opened cyanohydrin intermediate through sulfur chemistry, amination, and hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Dehydroxylated thioamide to arginine\",\n      \"description\": \"Experimentally observed late-stage route from the dehydroxylated thioamide to arginine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyanohydrin-guanidine terminal route\",\n      \"description\": \"Experimentally observed terminal route from 2-(4-cyano-4-hydroxybutyl)guanidine to arginine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_003\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_003\",\n  