d condition variants for formation of the cyclic intermediate. This node represents a second condition family; the balanced small-molecule stoichiometry is again a formal closure of the experimentally observed transformation, with sulfur written as S0 because the original report does not enumerate a complete net redox equation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu-assisted cyclization to the cyclic guanidine hemiaminal (variant C)\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"elemental copper\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-365K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"elemental copper\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"Geothermal wet-dry reheating regime; third literature-inspired Cu/H2S cyclization condition set with the same balanced net stoichiometry.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ring closure, hydrolysis, and reduction again generate the six-membered cyclic hemiaminal intermediate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This reaction encodes a third Cu/H2S-assisted cyclization variant consistent with Patel et al. (2015). As above, the transformation is experimental, while the explicit sulfur coproduct is a stoichiometric formalization introduced here because the publication reports the conversion but not a fully balanced elemental bookkeeping.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"HCN ring opening of the cyclic hemiaminal\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"HCN-rich surface pool; transient concentration and partial UV shielding favor productive cyanation.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide attacks the hemiaminal carbon, opening the ring and generating a linear cyanohydrin while retaining the guanidine unit.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This ring-opening/cyanation step is directly aligned with Patel et al. (2015). The product identity is literature-supported, though the review notes that the factors controlling the required regioselective opening are still not fully characterized.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrogen sulfide thiolysis of the nitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_010\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"Sulfide-rich cyanosulfidic pool or hot-spring runoff.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sulfide adds to the nitrile carbon and, after proton transfer, converts the nitrile into the corresponding thioamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Nitrile-to-thioamide conversion by H2S is a hallmark cyanosulfidic transformation and is experimentally supported in the arginine sequence of Patel et al. (2015). This step is one of the better-grounded sulfur-editing reactions in the network.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu-assisted reductive dehydroxylation of the hydroxy thioamide\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"elemental copper\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"elemental copper\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"Cu-bearing sulfide-rich surface under wet-dry cycling; sulfur is written as S0 for balanced redox bookkeeping.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cu-mediated reductive deoxygenation removes the beta-hydroxyl group and gives the saturated thioamide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) experimentally support Cu/H2S-driven dehydroxylation at this stage. The explicit elemental sulfur product is a balanced formalization of the reductive sulfide chemistry rather than a coproduct explicitly enumerated in the paper, but the underlying transformation is literature-backed.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Cu-assisted thioamide-to-cyanohydrin editing\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"elemental copper\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"elemental copper\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"HCN reintroduced on a sulfide-conditioned copper surface; sulfur extrusion is written as S0 for balance.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The thioamide undergoes hydrolytic editing and cyanation to regenerate a terminal cyanohydrin, while sulfur and ammonia are expelled.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Patel et al. (2015) report the functional conversion of this thioamide stage back to a nitrile/cyanohydrin intermediate under Cu/sulfide/HCN chemistry. Because the paper does not fully specify the net elemental bookkeeping, the explicit S0 byproduct used here is partly speculative and serves as a mass-balanced formalization of the experimentally supported transformation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Ammonolytic amination to the arginine aminonitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\"