"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Cu/H2S-mediated hydrolytic cyclization to 4-hydroxy-2-imino-hexahydropyrimidine", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_005", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_002", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["elemental copper"], "conditions": {"temperature": "310-340K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "elemental copper", "aqueous": true, "other": "Cu-bearing sulfide-rich aqueous surface pool; sulfur(0)-equivalent bookkeeping closes the redox balance under anoxic conditions."}, "mechanism": "Intramolecular attack of a guanidine nitrogen on the pendant nitrile closes a six-membered ring, and hydrolytic capture gives the C4 hemiaminal/hydroxylated cyclic intermediate.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) experimentally observed Cu/H2S-promoted cyclization of 2-(2-cyanoethyl)guanidine to the cyclic hydroxy-pyrimidine intermediate. The organic transformation is therefore literature-supported, but the paper's compact depiction does not fully specify inorganic sulfur/redox coproducts. This node uses a mass-balanced closure with H2S consumed and a sulfur(0)-equivalent byproduct; that sulfur sink is partly speculative and could in nature be Cu sulfide or polysulfide (Patel et al., 2015)."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Cu/H2S-mediated concentrated-film cyclization to 4-hydroxy-2-imino-hexahydropyrimidine", "inputs": ["mol_008", "mol_005", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_009", "mol_002", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["elemental copper"], "conditions": {"temperature": "330-360K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "elemental copper", "aqueous": false, "other": "Partially dried Cu-bearing mineral film with residual water during wet-dry cycling; concentrated hydrolytic cyclization rather than a fully dry shorthand variant."}, "mechanism": "The same intramolecular guanidine-to-nitrile cyclization occurs under lower water activity on a copper-bearing surface, with limited water still supplying the oxygen of the hemiaminal product.", "reasoning": "This node captures the lower-water-activity Cu/H2S cyclization regime consistent with the multiple cyclization variants reported by Patel et al. (2015), but avoids encoding a strictly dry variant because the minimal inorganic stoichiometry of that shorthand is underdetermined. As in rxn_002, the organic conversion is literature-supported while the sulfur(0)-equivalent bookkeeping is a redox-closed approximation for network balance (Patel et al., 2015)."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "HCN-mediated ring opening of the cyclic pyrimidine intermediate", "inputs": ["mol_009", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "HCN-rich shallow pool; transient shielding from prolonged UV favors survival of the reactive cyanohydrin product."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide attacks the cyclic hemiaminal carbon, opening the ring and furnishing a linear cyanohydrin-guanidine bearing a new nitrile handle.", "reasoning": "This ring-opening/cyanation step is directly part of the Patel arginine sequence. It is chemically important because it both linearizes the intermediate and extends the carbon framework, though regioselective opening at the productive site is one of the uncertainties noted for the network outside controlled laboratory conditions (Patel et al., 2015)."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Nitrile thiolysis to the hydroxy thioamide", "inputs": ["mol_010", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_011"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Sulfide-rich anoxic pool; rapid onward consumption helps protect the cyanohydrin-containing substrate from side reactions."}, "mechanism": "Hydrosulfide adds to the nitrile carbon and subsequent tautomerization/proton transfer yields the thioamide while preserving the neighboring hydroxyl group.", "reasoning": "This is a hallmark cyanosulfidic nitrile-to-thioamide transformation and is one of the best-supported sulfur-mediated steps in the Patel network. It preserves the functionality needed for later deoxygenation and reinstallation of the nitrile equivalent (Patel et al., 2015)."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Cu/H2S-mediated reductive dehydroxylation to the butanethioamide", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_005", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["elemental copper"], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-360K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "elemental copper", "aqueous": false, "other": "Low-water-activity copper-bearing surface during wet-dry cycling; sulfur(0)-equivalent bookkeeping used to close the redox step."}, "mechanism": "Copper-assisted reduction removes the secondary hydroxyl as water, producing the deoxygenated thioamide and linearizing the side-chain editing trajectory.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) report Cu/H2S-mediated dehydroxylation of the hydroxy-thioamide. The atom-balanced encoding here treats H2S as the net reductant and a sulfur(0)-equivalent as the inorganic sink. The organic deoxygenation is experimental, but the exact sulfur speciation under prebiotic copper chemistry remains unresolved and copper availability itself is a known environmental caveat (Patel et al., 2015)."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "HCN/water refunctionalization of the thioamide to the cyanohydrin guanidine", "inputs": ["mol_012", "mol_001", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_013", "mol_002", "mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["elemental copper"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "elemental copper", "aqueous": true, "other": "HCN pulse into a copper-bearing sulfide-rich pool; sulfur(0)-equivalent bookkeeping represents unresolved inorganic sulfur speciation."}, "mechanism": "The thioamide undergoes hydrolytic/cyanative editing that replaces the thioamide terminus with a cyanohydrin/nitrile equivalent while releasing ammonia.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) support this thioamide-to-cyanohydrin editing step under Cu/H2S/HCN/water conditions. Because the published shorthand is not fully atom-balanced, the present node preserves the supported organic conversion but uses release of NH3 and a sulfur(0)-equivalent for redox closure. Copper dependence and sulfur bookkeeping make this one of the more uncertain practical bottlenecks of the route (Patel et al., 2015)."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Ammonolysis of the cyanohydrin to the alpha-aminonitrile", "inputs": ["mol_013", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "NH3-rich concentrated pool; substitution is written with water as coproduct for atom-balanced bookkeeping."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia replaces the hydroxyl-bearing center of the cyanohydrin, giving the alpha-aminonitrile precursor to arginine and expelling water.", "reasoning": "This amination step is directly part of the Patel arginine sequence. Writing water as coproduct gives the chemically consistent substitution that earlier encodings often omitted. Concentrated ammonia in evaporitic pools would help drive conversion of the cyanohydrin to the alpha-aminonitrile (Patel et al., 2015)."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Strecker-type nitrile hydrolysis to arginine", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_015", "mol_002"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": null, "conditions": {"temperature": "320-370K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Warm aqueous episodes in anoxic pools; two water equivalents are inferred by balancing; product is expected to be racemic."}, "mechanism": "Stepwise hydration of the nitrile gives an amide and then the carboxylic acid, yielding arginine with release of ammonia.", "reasoning": "Patel et al. (2015) convert the 