m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Arginine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: Use the experimentally demonstrated surface cyanosulfidic core of the Patel et al. arginine sequence. The graph is rooted at 3-aminopropanenitrile, a compact late precursor that can itself arise upstream in the same chemistry, so the network stays within the requested size while preserving the literature-supported guanidination-to-arginine route. Cyanamide installs the guanidino precursor, Cu/H2S mediates cyclization to the hydroxyhexahydropyrimidine, HCN ring opening and sulfur-mediated thioamide editing build the full side chain, ammonia gives the alpha-aminonitrile, and aqueous hydrolysis yields racemic arginine. Only hydrolytic/concentrated cyclization variants are retained; the minimally specified dry shorthand is omitted, and Cu/H2S redox steps are written with an explicit sulfur(0)-equivalent byproduct so the network is atom-balanceable.\n\nMOLECULES (15 nodes):\n  - mol_001: HCN (hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_002: NH3 (ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_003: CH2N2 (cyanamide) [starting]\n  - mol_004: H2S (hydrogen sulfide) [starting]\n  - mol_005: H2O (water) [starting]\n  - mol_006: S (sulfur(0) equivalent) [intermediate]\n  - mol_007: C3H6N2 (3-aminopropionitrile) [starting]\n  - mol_008: C4H8N4 (2-(2-cy